AWSTemplateFormatVersion: "2010-09-09"

Description: >-
  Vocareum lab permission probe for the TEMPLATE DEPLOYER principal. Runs any
  list of AWS API calls inside the student account and reports which ones the
  organization ceiling permits, plus the raw AWS error for the ones it
  refuses. A Lambda-backed custom resource catches every error, so the stack
  reaches CREATE_COMPLETE and the diagnosis is readable as Outputs instead of
  as a rollback message. Read Summary and FirstFailureReason first.

# This is the deployer-side half of the Phase 0 instrument. The other half
# is vocareum-probe, which measures the student session role.
# Both use the same verdict vocabulary, so a result means the same thing
# whichever principal produced it. Neither can answer for the other.
#
# Deploy by uploading this file to /voc/private/lab.template in Configure
# Workspace. The filename must be exactly lab.template, so keep the real
# lab.template elsewhere while this probe is in place.
# vocareum-upload does the upload and verifies the bytes arrived
# intact.
#
# What this instrument can and cannot conclude:
#
#   - The probes run as the Lambda execution role this template creates, NOT
#     as vocareum/${LAB_DEPLOYER_ROLE} itself. That role's own policy is not
#     readable from inside the account.
#   - To stop the role's own policy from being the thing under test, the role
#     is granted Action "*". Anything that still fails therefore failed at the
#     organization ceiling or at the service, not at the role. That is the
#     whole point: grant everything, and whatever remains refused is the
#     ceiling. A grant of "*" is bounded by the SCP above it and disappears
#     when the stack is deleted.
#   - An SCP_DENY verdict is conclusive for every principal in the account,
#     the student session role included, because an SCP is a ceiling rather
#     than a grant.
#   - An OK verdict is NOT conclusive for the student session role. It proves
#     the ceiling allows the action. Whether Vocareum's own session-role
#     policy also allows it is a separate question, and vocareum-probe
#     is what answers it.
#   - If the stack fails on ProbeFunctionRole or ProbeFunction, the ceiling
#     blocks IAM or Lambda and every other question is still open. Set
#     ExecutionRoleArn to an existing role to drop the iam:CreateRole
#     dependency, then try again.
#   - No AWS::Logs::LogGroup is declared on purpose. Lambda creates its log
#     group lazily, so a logs deny costs the logs but still returns verdicts.
#     Declaring the group would let a logs deny fail the whole stack. The
#     price of that choice is that CloudFormation does not own the group and
#     will not delete it. Deleting the stack leaves
#     /aws/lambda/<generated-function-name> behind, so delete it by hand
#     afterwards. Verified on a real deploy, not assumed.

Parameters:
  Probes:
    Type: String
    Description: >-
      A JSON array of probe objects. Each needs "action" as an IAM action such
      as bedrock:ListFoundationModels, and may carry "kwargs" for the API call
      and "client" to override the boto3 client name. Keep the list short: a
      CloudFormation custom resource response is capped at 4096 bytes in
      total, and the template reports how many results it had to drop.
    Default: >-
      [{"action": "sts:GetCallerIdentity"},
       {"action": "bedrock:ListFoundationModels"},
       {"action": "lambda:ListFunctions"},
       {"action": "iam:ListRoles"},
       {"action": "dynamodb:ListTables"},
       {"action": "ecr:DescribeRepositories"},
       {"action": "codebuild:ListProjects"},
       {"action": "bedrock-agentcore:ListMemories", "client": "bedrock-agentcore-control"}]

  ExecutionRoleArn:
    Type: String
    Default: ""
    Description: >-
      Optional ARN of an existing role for the probe function to assume. Leave
      empty and the template creates one. Set it to an existing role, for
      example the LabRole that Vocareum-hosted accounts commonly provide, if
      the ceiling denies iam:CreateRole. The role must trust
      lambda.amazonaws.com.

Conditions:
  # The single knob that lets the probe be retried without iam:CreateRole.
  CreateExecutionRole: !Equals [!Ref ExecutionRoleArn, ""]

Resources:
  ProbeFunctionRole:
    Type: AWS::IAM::Role
    Condition: CreateExecutionRole
    Properties:
      AssumeRolePolicyDocument:
        Version: "2012-10-17"
        Statement:
          - Effect: Allow
            Principal:
              Service: lambda.amazonaws.com
            Action: sts:AssumeRole
      Policies:
        - PolicyName: measure-the-ceiling
          PolicyDocument:
            Version: "2012-10-17"
            Statement:
              # Deliberately unscoped. A least-privilege role here would make
              # the role's own policy the thing under test, and every denial
              # would be ambiguous between "the org forbids it" and "this
              # template forgot to allow it". Granting everything removes that
              # ambiguity: what still fails is the ceiling. The SCP above this
              # account still applies, and the role dies with the stack.
              - Sid: GrantEverythingSoTheCeilingIsWhatFails
                Effect: Allow
                Action: "*"
                Resource: "*"
      Tags:
        - Key: Purpose
          Value: vocareum-permission-probe
        - Key: StackName
          Value: !Ref AWS::StackName

  ProbeFunction:
    Type: AWS::Lambda::Function
    Properties:
      # No FunctionName is set, so CloudFormation generates one. A fixed name
      # would collide if a previous lab session failed to delete its stack,
      # and Vocareum resets labs often.
      Runtime: python3.13
      Handler: index.handler
      Role: !If
        - CreateExecutionRole
        - !GetAtt ProbeFunctionRole.Arn
        - !Ref ExecutionRoleArn
      # A Lambda that times out sends no response at all and leaves the stack
      # in CREATE_IN_PROGRESS for an hour, so the budget is generous relative
      # to a list of short API calls.
      Timeout: 300
      MemorySize: 512
      # Inline ZipFile only. The Vocareum flow has no build or upload step, so
      # an S3 artifact could never be staged.
      Code:
        ZipFile: |
          """Probe a list of AWS API calls and classify every outcome.

          Every probe is caught and classified. The handler always reports
          SUCCESS to CloudFormation and puts the diagnosis in the response
          data, so the stack completes and the verdicts are readable as stack
          Outputs instead of as a rollback message.

          The verdict vocabulary is shared verbatim with
          vocareum-probe. Changing one without the other makes results
          from the two principals stop being comparable, which is the only
          reason to run both.
          """

          import json
          import re
          from typing import Any

          import boto3
          import cfnresponse
          from botocore.config import Config
          from botocore.exceptions import BotoCoreError, ClientError

          # Short timeouts and few retries. A long probe list should not stall
          # on one hung socket, because a Lambda timeout means no response is
          # sent at all.
          BOTO_CONFIG = Config(
              connect_timeout=5,
              read_timeout=20,
              retries={"max_attempts": 2, "mode": "standard"},
          )

          # A custom resource response body is capped at 4096 bytes in total,
          # so every field is truncated before it goes in and the caller is
          # told how much was dropped.
          MAX_SUMMARY = 2400
          MAX_REASON = 900
          # Room held back so the "results were dropped" notice survives the
          # truncation it is reporting on.
          NOTICE_ROOM = 130

          # Ordered candidates, not one name. One IAM service prefix can be
          # served by more than one boto3 client, and which is right depends on
          # the operation. AgentCore management is on bedrock-agentcore-control
          # and InvokeAgentRuntime is on the bedrock-agentcore data plane. The
          # two share no operation name, so first match cannot pick wrong.
          # Kept identical to CLIENT_CANDIDATES in vocareum_tools/probe.py.
          CLIENT_CANDIDATES = {
              "bedrock-agentcore": ("bedrock-agentcore-control", "bedrock-agentcore"),
              "elasticloadbalancing": ("elbv2",),
          }

          TRACKER_STATUS = {
              "OK": "Allowed",
              "ALLOWED_NOT_FOUND": "Allowed",
              "THROTTLED": "Allowed",
              "QUOTA_EXCEEDED": "Allowed",
              "MODEL_ACCESS_NOT_GRANTED": "Allowed",
              "MODEL_LEGACY_INACTIVE": "Allowed",
              "MODEL_USE_CASE_NOT_SUBMITTED": "Allowed",
              "MODEL_NOT_IN_REGION": "Allowed",
              "SCP_DENY": "Denied (SCP)",
              "RCP_DENY": "Denied (SCP)",
              "IAM_DENY": "Denied (IAM)",
              "ACCESS_DENIED_UNCLASSIFIED": "Denied (IAM)",
              "OPT_IN_REQUIRED": "Blocked",
          }

          DENIAL_CODES = {
              "AccessDenied",
              "AccessDeniedException",
              "AuthorizationError",
              "AccessDeniedError",
          }

          NOT_FOUND_CODES = {
              "ResourceNotFoundException",
              "ResourceNotFound",
              "NoSuchEntity",
              "NoSuchEntityException",
              "NoSuchBucket",
              "NoSuchKey",
              "EntityNotFoundException",
              "NotFoundException",
          }

          # Request-shape validation is NOT evidence of authorization. A
          # malformed or unknown parameter is rejected before the
          # authorization check ever runs, measured against a principal
          # holding AdministratorAccess. Treating these as Allowed would
          # manufacture permissions that were never tested.
          INCONCLUSIVE_CODES = {
              "ValidationException",
              "ValidationError",
              "InvalidParameterValue",
              "InvalidParameterValueException",
              "InvalidParameterCombination",
              "MissingParameter",
          }

          MAPPED_CODES = {
              "ThrottlingException": "THROTTLED",
              "Throttling": "THROTTLED",
              "TooManyRequestsException": "THROTTLED",
              "RequestLimitExceeded": "THROTTLED",
              "ServiceQuotaExceededException": "QUOTA_EXCEEDED",
              "LimitExceededException": "QUOTA_EXCEEDED",
              "LimitExceeded": "QUOTA_EXCEEDED",
              "OptInRequired": "OPT_IN_REQUIRED",
              "SubscriptionRequiredException": "OPT_IN_REQUIRED",
              "UnrecognizedClientException": "CREDENTIALS_BROKEN",
              "InvalidClientTokenId": "CREDENTIALS_BROKEN",
              "InvalidSignatureException": "CREDENTIALS_BROKEN",
              "ExpiredToken": "CREDENTIALS_BROKEN",
              "ExpiredTokenException": "CREDENTIALS_BROKEN",
          }

          # AWS names the refused action in the message body. Reading it back
          # is how a denial for a dependency gets told apart from a denial for
          # the action actually being probed. The action half admits ``*``
          # because a policy denial can name a wildcard. Kept identical to
          # vocareum_tools/aws.py, so both harnesses read a denial the same way.
          DENIED_ACTION = re.compile(
              r"not authorized to perform:?\s*([A-Za-z0-9-]+):([A-Za-z0-9_*]+)"
          )


          def clip(text: Any, limit: int) -> str:
              """Collapse whitespace and trim, marking any truncation."""
              collapsed = " ".join(str(text).split())
              if len(collapsed) <= limit:
                  return collapsed
              return collapsed[: limit - 3] + "..."


          def names_other_action(message: str, probed: str) -> bool:
              """Report whether a denial names a single action other than this one.

              A wildcard is not a different action. A policy that refuses
              ``bedrock:*`` refuses the action being measured along with
              everything else, so the denial belongs to this measurement and
              has to be classified as a denial rather than set aside as
              somebody else's.
              """
              match = DENIED_ACTION.search(message)
              if match is None or not probed:
                  return False
              named = f"{match.group(1)}:{match.group(2)}"
              if "*" in named:
                  return False
              return named.lower() != probed.lower()


          def method_name(operation: str) -> str:
              """Convert an IAM action name to its boto3 method name."""
              step = re.sub(r"(.)([A-Z][a-z]+)", r"\1_\2", operation)
              return re.sub(r"([a-z0-9])([A-Z])", r"\1_\2", step).lower()


          def classify(error: ClientError, probed: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
              """Map a ClientError onto a verdict and its raw message.

              The order inside the denial branch is load bearing. AWS names
              the blocking policy type in the message body, and that string is
              the only reliable way to tell an organization ceiling apart from
              a gap in the account's own IAM. Both arrive as AccessDenied.
              """
              info = error.response.get("Error", {})
              code = str(info.get("Code", "Unknown"))
              message = str(info.get("Message", str(error)))
              lowered = message.lower()

              if code in DENIAL_CODES:
                  # A denial naming a different action says nothing about this
                  # one. Checked only within a denial, because outside one the
                  # same wording can appear in text that is not a refusal.
                  if names_other_action(message, probed):
                      return "DEPENDENCY_DENIED", message
                  if "service control policy" in lowered:
                      return "SCP_DENY", message
                  if "resource control policy" in lowered:
                      return "RCP_DENY", message
                  if "not authorized to perform" in lowered:
                      return "IAM_DENY", message
                  if "access to the model" in lowered:
                      return "MODEL_ACCESS_NOT_GRANTED", message
                  return "ACCESS_DENIED_UNCLASSIFIED", message

              # Bedrock also uses ResourceNotFoundException when a legacy
              # model is unavailable to an account that did not use it
              # recently. That is a lifecycle block, not a Region problem.
              if code == "ResourceNotFoundException" and (
                  "marked by provider as legacy" in lowered
                  or ("legacy" in lowered and "last 30 days" in lowered)
              ):
                  return "MODEL_LEGACY_INACTIVE", message
              if (
                  code == "ResourceNotFoundException"
                  and "use case details" in lowered
              ):
                  return "MODEL_USE_CASE_NOT_SUBMITTED", message

              # Bedrock reuses ResourceNotFoundException to mean "that model
              # is not in this region", which is worth telling apart from an
              # ordinary missing resource even though both land on Allowed.
              # lab-bedrock.template records the same mapping.
              #
              # The colon is load-bearing. Matching the bare prefix also
              # caught bedrock-agentcore, which has no models in it, so every
              # absent gateway, runtime, or memory was reported as a model
              # missing from this Region.
              if code == "ResourceNotFoundException" and probed.startswith(
                  "bedrock:"
              ):
                  return "MODEL_NOT_IN_REGION", message
              if code in NOT_FOUND_CODES:
                  return "ALLOWED_NOT_FOUND", message
              if code in INCONCLUSIVE_CODES:
                  return "INCONCLUSIVE_BAD_INPUT", message
              return MAPPED_CODES.get(code, "UNCLASSIFIED"), message


          def run_probe(region: str, probe: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, str]:
              """Run one probe and classify whatever comes back."""
              action = str(probe.get("action", ""))
              if ":" not in action:
                  return {
                      "action": action or "(unnamed)",
                      "verdict": "UNKNOWN_ACTION",
                      "detail": "Not an IAM action. Write it as service:Action.",
                  }

              service, operation = action.split(":", 1)
              pinned = probe.get("client")
              candidates = (
                  (pinned,) if pinned else CLIENT_CANDIDATES.get(service, (service,))
              )

              wanted = method_name(operation)
              method = None
              unbuildable = []
              for client_name in candidates:
                  try:
                      client = boto3.client(
                          client_name, region_name=region, config=BOTO_CONFIG
                      )
                  except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
                      unbuildable.append(f"no boto3 client named {client_name}: {error}")
                      continue
                  method = getattr(client, wanted, None)
                  if method is not None:
                      break

              if method is None:
                  detail = f"no client among {', '.join(candidates)} has {wanted}"
                  if unbuildable:
                      detail = f"{detail}; {'; '.join(unbuildable)}"
                  return {
                      "action": action,
                      "verdict": "UNKNOWN_ACTION",
                      "detail": detail,
                  }

              try:
                  method(**probe.get("kwargs", {}))
              except ClientError as error:
                  verdict, message = classify(error, action)
                  return {
                      "action": action,
                      "verdict": verdict,
                      "detail": message,
                  }
              except BotoCoreError as error:
                  return {
                      "action": action,
                      "verdict": "ENDPOINT_UNREACHABLE",
                      "detail": str(error),
                  }
              return {"action": action, "verdict": "OK", "detail": "call succeeded"}


          def probed_principal(region: str) -> str:
              """Report which principal the probes actually ran as."""
              try:
                  who = boto3.client(
                      "sts", region_name=region, config=BOTO_CONFIG
                  ).get_caller_identity()
              except (ClientError, BotoCoreError) as error:
                  return f"unknown: {error}"
              return str(who.get("Arn", "unknown"))


          def run_all(region: str, probes: list[dict[str, Any]]) -> dict[str, str]:
              """Run every probe and fold the results into stack outputs."""
              results = [run_probe(region, probe) for probe in probes]

              lines = []
              for item in results:
                  status = TRACKER_STATUS.get(item["verdict"], "Untested")
                  lines.append(f"{item['action']}={item['verdict']}[{status}]")

              summary = " ".join(lines)
              dropped = 0
              if len(summary) > MAX_SUMMARY:
                  # Drop down to a budget that leaves room for the notice
                  # itself, otherwise the final clip eats the very sentence
                  # that admits results went missing and the output reads as
                  # if everything was covered.
                  while lines and len(" ".join(lines)) > MAX_SUMMARY - NOTICE_ROOM:
                      lines.pop()
                      dropped += 1
                  summary = " ".join(lines) + (
                      f" ...{dropped} more result(s) dropped to fit the 4096 "
                      "byte response cap. The log stream has all of them."
                  )

              denied = [
                  item
                  for item in results
                  if TRACKER_STATUS.get(item["verdict"], "Untested").startswith(
                      "Denied"
                  )
              ]
              first = denied[0] if denied else None

              counts: dict[str, int] = {}
              for item in results:
                  status = TRACKER_STATUS.get(item["verdict"], "Untested")
                  counts[status] = counts.get(status, 0) + 1

              return {
                  "Summary": clip(summary, MAX_SUMMARY),
                  "Counts": " ".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in sorted(counts.items())),
                  "ProbeCount": str(len(results)),
                  "FirstFailureAction": first["action"] if first else "none",
                  "FirstFailureReason": (
                      clip(first["detail"], MAX_REASON) if first else "No denials."
                  ),
                  "ProbedPrincipal": clip(probed_principal(region), 300),
                  "Region": region,
              }


          def handler(event: dict[str, Any], context: Any) -> None:
              """Run the probes and always answer CloudFormation."""
              print(json.dumps({"RequestType": event.get("RequestType")}))
              data: dict[str, str] = {}
              try:
                  if event.get("RequestType") == "Delete":
                      cfnresponse.send(
                          event, context, cfnresponse.SUCCESS, {},
                          physicalResourceId="permission-probe",
                      )
                      return
                  props = event.get("ResourceProperties", {})
                  probes = json.loads(props.get("Probes", "[]"))
                  if not isinstance(probes, list):
                      raise ValueError("Probes must be a JSON array.")
                  data = run_all(props["Region"], probes)
                  # The log stream carries every result untruncated, which is
                  # the escape hatch when the response cap drops some.
                  print(json.dumps({"probes": probes}))
                  print(json.dumps(data))
              except Exception as error:  # noqa: BLE001
                  # Deliberate catch-all, and the one place a broad except is
                  # correct here. Anything escaping this frame would leave
                  # CloudFormation waiting an hour for a response that never
                  # arrives, which is the exact failure mode this template
                  # exists to avoid.
                  data = {
                      "Summary": "PROBE_ERROR",
                      "Counts": "",
                      "ProbeCount": "0",
                      "FirstFailureAction": "probe harness",
                      "FirstFailureReason": clip(
                          f"{type(error).__name__}: {error}", MAX_REASON
                      ),
                      "ProbedPrincipal": "unknown",
                      "Region": str(
                          event.get("ResourceProperties", {}).get("Region", "")
                      ),
                  }
              cfnresponse.send(
                  event, context, cfnresponse.SUCCESS, data,
                  physicalResourceId="permission-probe",
              )
      Tags:
        - Key: Purpose
          Value: vocareum-permission-probe
        - Key: StackName
          Value: !Ref AWS::StackName

  PermissionProbe:
    Type: AWS::CloudFormation::CustomResource
    Properties:
      ServiceToken: !GetAtt ProbeFunction.Arn
      # Passed as properties rather than read from the environment so that a
      # stack update with a different probe list re-runs the probes.
      Region: !Ref AWS::Region
      Probes: !Ref Probes

Outputs:
  Summary:
    Description: >-
      One entry per probe as action=VERDICT[Status]. Status is the value to
      copy into the ceiling table in docs/permissions.md.
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.Summary

  Counts:
    Description: How many probes landed in each tracker Status
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.Counts

  ProbeCount:
    Description: How many probes ran
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.ProbeCount

  FirstFailureAction:
    Description: The first probe that came back denied, or none
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.FirstFailureAction

  FirstFailureReason:
    Description: >-
      Raw AWS error message behind the first denial. An SCP deny names the
      offending policy id here, which is what Vocareum support will ask for.
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.FirstFailureReason

  ProbedPrincipal:
    Description: >-
      The role the probes ran as. This is the probe function's execution role,
      not vocareum/${LAB_DEPLOYER_ROLE} and not the student session role. An
      SCP deny applies to every principal in the account and is conclusive. An
      OK proves the org ceiling allows the action, and nothing more.
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.ProbedPrincipal

  Region:
    Description: The region the probes ran in
    Value: !GetAtt PermissionProbe.Region

  LogsLink:
    Description: >-
      CloudWatch log group for the probe function. Every result is written
      there untruncated, which is where to look when the 4096 byte response
      cap drops some.
    Value: !Sub https://${AWS::Region}.console.aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/home?region=${AWS::Region}#logsV2:log-groups/log-group/$252Faws$252Flambda$252F${ProbeFunction}
